lang=\"EN-GB\">STAT3</span></em><span lang=\"EN-GB\"> mutations are found in as many as 42% of cases, particularly in patients with HIV and EBV, involving the SH2 domain and associated with increased phosphorylated STAT3 expression </span><span class=\"Ref-PMID\" style=\"color: #984806; background: #ccc0d9;\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">{32273478}</span></span><span lang=\"EN-GB\"> and JAK/STAT pathway signature overexpression </span><span class=\"Ref-PMID\" style=\"color: #984806; background: #ccc0d9;\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">{33951889; 33225311}</span></span><span lang=\"EN-GB\">. Other JAK/STAT pathway components such as <em>JAK1</em> and <em>SOCS1</em> are also recurrently mutated in 5&ndash;15% of the cases </span><span class=\"Ref-PMID\" style=\"color: #984806; background: #ccc0d9;\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">{33951889; 33225311; 34465776}</span></span><span lang=\"EN-GB\">. MAPK/ERK pathway mutations include <em>NRAS</em> (30%), <em>KRAS</em> (12%), <em>HRAS</em> (2%), <em>BRAF</em> (7%), and <em>MAP2K1</em> (7%) mutations. Other genetic events include <em>TP53</em> mutation, 17p&nbsp;deletion&nbsp;/ loss of heterozygosity, <em>IRF4</em> (6p25.3) amplification (29%) </span><span class=\"Ref-PMID\" style=\"color: #984806; background: #ccc0d9;\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">{34465776}</span></span><span lang=\"EN-GB\">, 11p13 gain (including <em>CD44</em>) (37%) </span><span class=\"Ref-PMID\" style=\"color: #984806; background: #ccc0d9;\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">{33225311}</span></span><span lang=\"EN-GB\">, and 1q21.3 gain (including <em>MCL1</em>, <em>IL6R</em>) (43%) </span><span class=\"Ref-PMID\" style=\"color: #984806; background: #ccc0d9;\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">{33225311; 34465776}</span></span><span lang=\"EN-GB\">.</span></p>",
